Closures with setTimeout in a for Loop

1. var — All callbacks share the same variable

Example

for (var i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {

    setTimeout(function () {
        console.log(i);
    }, 1000);

}

Output
4
4
4

How it works

var is function-scoped, so the loop has only one i variable.

During the loop:

Iteration 1 → i = 1 → callback 1 created
Iteration 2 → i = 2 → callback 2 created
Iteration 3 → i = 3 → callback 3 created

After the third iteration:

i++

makes:

i = 4

Then:

4 <= 3

is false, so the loop ends.

The callbacks haven't executed yet because setTimeout schedules them for later.

After approximately 1 second, the callbacks execute.

All three callbacks have a closure over the same i variable:

callback 1 ──┐
callback 2 ──┼──→ same i → 4
callback 3 ──┘

Therefore:

4
4
4

Important point

A closure captures the variable/binding, not a snapshot of its value at the moment the callback was created.
